      "title": "Page 3",
      "pageStart": 3,
      "pageEnd": 3,
      "target": "../sections/page-003.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "3\n\nequal number of oppositely signed crossings is emphasised in Moffatt (1981)\nwho draws attention to the striking example of the Borromean rings. This\nvery old heraldic device is used on the coat of arms of the Borromeo family\nof Italian noblility. It comprises three linked rings, no two of which are linked\n(see figure la). The Gauss integral taken over any two rings vanishes and\nso the helicity of the flux tube configuration is zero. The subtle intertwining\nof the Borromean rings may however be described by a third order linking\nintegral as shown in Berger (1990, 1991) and reviewed in sectio",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "not-summarised"
